groundHeight:

```js
function groundHeight(x, z) {
  let h = Math.sin(x * 0.045) * 1.4 + Math.cos(z * 0.05) * 1.2 + Math.sin((x + z) * 0.028) * 1.6;
  h += gauss(x, z, -60, 10, 30) * 5;
  h += gauss(x, z, 25, -15, 40) * 4;
  h += gauss(x, z, 115, -35, 20) * 6;
  h -= gauss(x, z, 88, 0, 7) * 3;
  return h;
}
function gauss(x, z, cx, cz, s) {
  const dx = x - cx, dz = z - cz;
  return Math.exp(-(dx * dx + dz * dz) / (2 * s * s));
}
```

Terrain:

```js
const terraGeometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(300, 220, 140, 100);
terraGeometry.rotateX(-Math.PI / 2);
const pos = terraGeometry.attributes.position;
const colors = [];
const c1 = new THREE.Color(0x6d7c3e), c2 = new THREE.Color(0x8a9a52), c3 = new THREE.Color(0x5b6b33);
for (let i = 0; i < pos.count; i++) {
  const x = pos.getX(i), z = pos.getZ(i);
  const h = groundHeight(x, z);
  pos.setY(i, h);
  const t = THREE.MathUtils.clamp((h + 2) / 10, 0, 1);
  const col = c2.clone().lerp(c1, t);
  if (x > 80) col.lerp(c3, 0.35);
  colors.push(col.r, col.g, col.b);
}
terraGeometry.setAttribute('color', new THREE.Float32BufferAttribute(colors, 3));
terraGeometry.computeVertexNormals();
const terrain = new THREE.Mesh(terraGeometry, new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ vertexColors: true, roughness: 1 }));
terrain.receiveShadow = true;
scene.add(terrain);
```

Wait — PlaneGeometry rotated -90 around X: after rotation, the plane's local Y becomes world Z... Actually the attribute positions after rotateX(-PI/2): original plane is in XY plane; rotating -PI/2 about X maps (x, y, 0) to (x, 0, y*?) Let me think: rotateX(-PI/2) transforms y -> y*cos(-90) - z*sin(-90) = z... For a point (x, y, z): rotation about X by angle a: y' = y*cos(a) - z*sin(a); z' = y*sin(a) + z*cos(a). With a=-PI/2: cos=0, sin=-1. y' = y*0 - z*(-1) = z; z' = y*(-1) + z*0 = -y. So (x, y, 0) → (x, 0, -y). So plane extends in X and Z (Z = -y). After rotation, geometry positions have y=0, z spread. Then setting pos.setY(i, h) works and pos.getZ gives world z directly. Good.

