wallSeg(x1, z1, x2, z2, h, thick = 2, mat): oriented box between two points:

```js
function wallSeg(x1, z1, x2, z2, h, thick = 2, mat = wallMat) {
  const dx = x2 - x1, dz = z2 - z1;
  const len = Math.hypot(dx, dz);
  const m = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(len, h, thick), mat);
  const base = Math.min(groundHeight(x1, z1), groundHeight(x2, z2)) - 0.5;
  m.position.set((x1 + x2) / 2, base + h / 2, (z1 + z2) / 2);
  m.rotation.y = -Math.atan2(dz, dx);
  m.castShadow = true;
  m.receiveShadow = true;
  scene.add(m);
}
```

Rotation: box's length along local X. To align local X with direction (dx, dz), rotation.y = -atan2(dz, dx). Yes.

archFrame(w, h, t, d, mat): wall with horseshoe arch opening:

```js
function archFrame(w, h, t, d, mat = wallMat) {
  const shape = new THREE.Shape();
  shape.moveTo(-w / 2, 0);
  shape.lineTo(w / 2, 0);
  shape.lineTo(w / 2, h);
  shape.lineTo(-w / 2, h);
  shape.closePath();
  const r = w * 0.42;
  const hole = new THREE.Path();
  hole.moveTo(-w * 0.26, 0);
  hole.absarc(0, h * 0.42, r, Math.PI, 0, true);
  hole.lineTo(w * 0.26, 0);
  hole.closePath();
  shape.holes.push(hole);
  const g = new THREE.ExtrudeGeometry(shape, { depth: d, bevelEnabled: false });
  const m = new THREE.Mesh(g, mat);
  m.castShadow = true;
  return m;
}
```

Wait — the hole path: absarc(0, y0, r, Math.PI, 0, true) draws from angle PI (point (-r, y0)) to angle 0 (point (r, y0)), clockwise=true means... In Three.js, absarc with clockwise=true goes from startAngle decreasing to endAngle, i.e. from PI down to 0 going through PI/2 (top)? Clockwise in standard math orientation (counterclockwise positive) means decreasing angle: PI → PI/2 → 0 passes through the top (y0 + r). Hmm: angle PI/2 is (0, r) which is the TOP of the circle. Going from PI to 0 the short way through PI/2 is the top arc — that's counterclockwise-in-decreasing-angle... wait.

Points on circle: angle PI = (-r, 0); angle 0 = (r, 0). Path via top: (0, +r). Going from angle PI to angle 0 through PI/2 means angle decreasing from PI to 0 through PI/2 — yes passes top. `clockwise=true` in absarc means aClockwise=true draws the arc in clockwise direction, which decreases angle. From PI decreasing to 0 passes PI/2 = top. 

So the arc bulges upward — correct for an arch. Then hole.lineTo(w*0.26, 0)? The arc ends at (r, y0) = (w*0.42, h*0.42). Hmm, hole side walls at ±w*0.26 but arc radius 0.42w > 0.26w mismatch — the Path will be auto-closed: after absarc ends at (0.42w, y0), closePath connects back to start (-0.26w, 0)? Path auto-close: when triangulated, the path is closed from last point to first. The last point of the arc is (r, y0) and first point is (-w*0.26, 0). The closing line is a diagonal — bad.

Fix: make side walls at x = ±r (i.e., straight sides under the arc endpoints). Let me design: hole start at (-r, 0), absarc(0, y0, r, PI, 0, true) → ends at (r, y0), then lineTo(r, 0) down, closePath → back to start (-r, 0). That's a clean outline: left wall up, top arc, right wall down. Wait but that means hole width = 2r = 0.84w at springline and the straight walls only reach y0... Let me re-think the shape of a horseshoe-arch gate: opening is wider at top? A horseshoe arch is narrower at bottom. Simpler: opening = vertical-sided arch with round top: sides at ±w*0.28 from 0 up to h*0.42, then semicircle above. For horseshoe feel, radius slightly bigger than side half-width:

